    {
        "title": "SEO vs Google Ads: Which Is Better for Small Businesses?",
        "slug": "seo-vs-google-ads",
        "category_slug": "seo-optimization",
        "featured_image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1432821596592-e2c18b78144f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80",
        "tags": "SEO, Google Ads, PPC, Small Business",
        "is_featured": False,
        "meta_title": "SEO vs Google Ads: Which Is Better for Small Businesses?",
        "meta_description": "SEO is slow and compounding; Google Ads is instant and paid. A plain-language comparison to help small businesses decide where to spend their next rupee.",
        "content": _html(
            "<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Neither is 'better' â€” they solve different problems. <strong>Google Ads</strong> puts you at the top of results within days, but every click costs money and stops the moment you pause it. <strong>SEO</strong> is slow (3â€“6 months to meaningful results) but compounding and effectively free per click once it works. Most small businesses do best with a small ads budget while SEO builds underneath.</p>",
            "<h2>The Real Difference: Speed vs Compounding</h2>",
            "<ul><li><strong>Google Ads:</strong> instant visibility, precise control of budget, measurable cost per click. The cost of a lead is always visible â€” and always payable.</li><li><strong>SEO:</strong> no per-click cost, but a long build. Once a page ranks, it keeps sending traffic month after month without extra payment. That's the compounding part.</li></ul>",
            "<h2>When Google Ads Makes More Sense</h2>",
            "<ul><li><strong>Time-sensitive offers:</strong> a launch, a festival sale, a limited-time service.</li><li><strong>New businesses with no history:</strong> you need customers now while SEO slowly builds.</li><li><strong>Competitive local terms:</strong> when every competitor is already in the map pack, ads get you in front immediately.</li><li><strong>Testable offers:</strong> ads tell you within weeks whether an offer works.</li></ul>",
            "<h2>When SEO Makes More Sense</h2>",
            "<ul><li><strong>Long-term economics:</strong> once ranking, the cost per lead falls far below ads.</li><li><strong>Low budget:</strong> SEO doesn't need ad spend, just consistent effort.</li><li><strong>Educational content:</strong> questions your customers research before buying are won by content, not ads.</li><li><strong>Brand trust:</strong> organic results carry a credibility ads don't.</li></ul>",
            "<h2>What Small Businesses Usually Get Wrong</h2>",
            "<p>The common failure isn't choosing wrong â€” it's abandoning one mid-build. SEO abandons after two months because there are no results yet (there wouldn't be). Ads get paused every time cash tightens, so they never build data or optimisation history. The business ends up paying for both and profiting from neither.</p>",
            "<p>A cleaner pattern: use ads to generate cash flow and learn what converts, and run SEO continuously beneath. For Delhi small businesses especially, <a href=\"/blog/local-seo-for-businesses-in-delhi/\">local SEO</a> plus a modest ads budget is often the highest-ROI combination.</p>",
            "<h2>Costs You Should Actually Expect</h2>",
            "<ul><li><strong>Google Ads:</strong> your click budget (typical small-business starting budgets run â‚¹500â€“â‚¹5,000/day depending on competition) plus the person managing it. Poorly-managed accounts waste budget fast â€” see our <a href=\"/blog/how-long-does-seo-take/\">SEO timeline guide</a> for the counterpart.</li><li><strong>SEO:</strong> our <a href=\"/services/seo-optimization/\">SEO service starts at â‚¹3,999/month</a>; the main hidden cost is consistency over months.</li></ul>",
            "<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>",
            "<h3>Can I do SEO and Google Ads together?</h3>",
            "<p>Yes, and it's usually the right answer. Ads cover you now; SEO covers you later. Just treat them as separate budgets with separate goals.</p>",
            "<h3>Is Google Ads more expensive than SEO?</h3>",
            "<p>Per month, yes â€” ads are cash out every month, indefinitely. SEO has lower cash cost but higher effort. Over two years, well-run SEO is almost always cheaper per lead.</p>",
            "<h3>My competitor ranks first organically. Should I compete with SEO or ads?</h3>",
            "<p>Ask how long they've been at it. If it's years, ads can still get you in front today. Don't assume a page-one rival is unbeatable â€” but also don't assume you'll overtake them in a month.</p>",
            "<p>Not sure where to start? <a href=\"/consultation/book/\">Book a free audit</a> and we'll map the honest path â€” including cases where the answer is 'fix your website first'.</p>",
        ),
    },
    {
        "title": "Technical SEO Checklist for Business Websites",
        "slug": "technical-seo-checklist",
        "category_slug": "seo-optimization",
        "featured_image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80",
        "tags": "Technical SEO, Checklist, Website Health",
        "is_featured": False,
        "meta_title": "Technical SEO Checklist for Business Websites",
        "meta_description": "A 12-point technical SEO checklist every business website needs â€” crawlability, indexing, canonical URLs, HTTPS, speed, mobile, and structured data.",
        "content": _html(
            "<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Technical SEO makes sure Google can find, crawl, understand, and index your website efficiently. The essentials: a crawlable structure, an XML sitemap, correct canonical URLs, HTTPS, fast page loads, mobile-friendliness, no duplicate-content traps, and structured data. Skip these and no amount of content or links will rank â€” search engines can't reward pages they can't process.</p>",
            "<h2>Why Technical SEO Is the Foundation</h2>",
            "<p>Think of technical SEO as the plumbing. Content is the tap water; if the pipes are blocked, nothing reaches the sink. Businesses that fix technical issues often see indexing improve within weeks â€” it's the fastest-paying SEO work and the least glamorous. That's why it's the first step in our <a href=\"/services/seo-optimization/\">SEO process</a>.</p>",
            "<h2>Checklist: Run These On Your Site</h2>",
            "<h3>1. Crawlability</h3>",
            "<p>Can search engines reach every important page? Check your <code>robots.txt</code> isn't blocking real pages, there are no 'crawl traps' (URLs that spawn infinite variants), and navigation is HTML links, not only JavaScript.</p>",
            "<h3>2. XML Sitemap</h3>",
            "<p>Submit a sitemap listing your important pages â€” it's a strong hint about what matters on your site. Verify it in Google Search Console.</p>",
            "<h3>3. Canonical URLs</h3>",
            "<p>Every page should state its canonical version. This prevents the classic duplicate-content problem where the same page is reachable at multiple URLs and Google picks the wrong one â€” or splits your ranking signals.</p>",
            "<h3>4. HTTPS</h3>",
            "<p>Your whole site should run on HTTPS with a valid certificate. Google flags insecure sites, and visitors trust the padlock.</p>",
            "<h3>5. Indexing Coverage</h3>",
            "<p>In Search Console, check how many of your submitted pages are actually indexed, and what's blocking the rest (noindex, canonical issues, or pages that were never found).</p>",
            "<h3>6. Page Speed</h3>",
            "<p>Speed is a ranking factor and a conversion factor. Compress images, use caching, and minify code. A 2-second page outperforms a 6-second one â€” on rankings and on enquiries.</p>",
            "<h3>7. Mobile Friendliness</h3>",
            "<p>Most Indian business searches happen on phones. Test your site at actual phone widths; the content and forms must work, not just 'fit'. Our <a href=\"/services/website-development/\">websites are mobile-first</a> for exactly this reason.</p>",
            "<h3>8. Structured Data (Schema)</h3>",
            "<p>Schema helps Google understand your content â€” business type, address, hours, articles, FAQs, products. It's not a magic ranking switch, but it enables rich results and improves how your pages are understood. Used consistently and correctly, it's part of how we build sites.</p>",
            "<h3>9. Clean URL Structure</h3>",
            "<p>Descriptive, readable URLs (<code>/services/website-development/</code>) beat query-string noise (<code>/?p=123&amp;cat=4</code>). Keep them stable â€” changing URLs breaks links and rankings.</p>",
            "<h3>10. Internal Links</h3>",
            "<p>Link between your own pages with descriptive anchor text. It distributes authority and helps Google understand your site's structure â€” like the related-article links you'll find across our <a href=\"/blog/\">insights</a>.</p>",
            "<h3>11. Handle Errors Gracefully</h3>",
            "<p>Broken links should return a proper 404 page (not a soft 200), and moved pages should 301-redirect to their new location.</p>",
            "<h3>12. No Duplicate or Thin Content</h3>",
            "<p>Every indexable page should exist for a reason. Near-identical pages with only the city swapped are a doorway-page risk â€” the reason we deliberately avoid building <a href=\"/locations/web-development-delhi/\">duplicate city pages</a>.</p>",
            "<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>",
            "<h3>Do I need a technical SEO expert, or can I do this myself?</h3>",
            "<p>The basics â€” sitemap, Search Console, HTTPS, speed tests â€” are DIY-able. Deeper issues (canonical mistakes, crawl traps, schema errors) are where an expert earns their fee. Our <a href=\"/services/seo-optimization/\">SEO service</a> starts at â‚¹3,999/month and includes the technical layer.</p>",
            "<h3>Is technical SEO more important than content?</h3>",
            "<p>They're different stages of the same job. Technical SEO gets your pages crawlable and indexed; content wins the rankings. A technically clean site with weak content ranks for little; a content-rich site that can't be crawled ranks for nothing.</p>",
            "<h3>Will fixing technical SEO alone improve rankings?</h3>",
            "<p>It removes the barriers â€” that alone can help indexing and reveal pages Google couldn't see. But rankings still need relevance, content, and links on top. For a deeper look at the technical side in a Django context, see our <a href=\"/blog/technical-seo-checklist-for-sub-300ms-django-page-speeds/\">Django performance &amp; SEO article</a>.</p>",
            "<p>Want a technical audit of your own site? <a href=\"/consultation/book/\">Book a free audit</a> and we'll walk you through exactly what's blocking you.</p>",
        ),
    },
